# ${{ values.apiName }}

${{ values.description }}

This repository deploys the API to [Cloud Endpoints](https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs) in the Google Cloud project `${{ values.projectId }}`. The API is described in `${{ values.grpcSpec if grpc else values.openApiSpec }}`{% if grpc %} and `api_config.yaml`{% endif %}, the infrastructure in Terraform under `infra/`, and GitHub Actions checks and deploys both.

## What is deployed

| Resource | Name | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Endpoints service | `${{ serviceName }}` | Holds the API configuration: {% if grpc %}the gRPC services{% else %}the operations{% endif %}, the backend address{% if authType != 'none' %}, the authentication rules{% endif %}{% if values.enableQuotas %}, the quota{% endif %} |
| Project services | Endpoints, Service Management, Service Control, Cloud Run | APIs the service and its gateway need; the Endpoints service itself is enabled as well |
| Service account | `${{ values.apiName | truncate(22, true, '') }}-gateway` | Identity of the gateway |
| Cloud Run service | `${{ values.apiName }}-gateway` | Runs the Extensible Service Proxy (ESPv2), the gateway clients call |
{%- if values.enableMonitoring %}
| Monitoring dashboard | `${{ values.apiName }} API` | Request rate by response class and by method, latency |
| Alert policies | `${{ values.apiName }}: 5xx responses`, `${{ values.apiName }}: slow responses` | Fire on the 5xx ratio and on the 95th percentile latency |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableLogging %}
| Log bucket and sink | `${{ values.apiName }}-api-logs` | Store the request logs of the API and the logs of the gateway |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enablePortal %}
| IAM members on the service | from the `api_consumers` variable | Principals that may enable the API in their own projects |
{%- endif %}

## How a request is handled

1. A client calls the URL of the gateway (the `gateway_url` Terraform output, a `run.app` address).
{%- if apiKey and authType != 'none' and authType != 'apikey' %}
2. ESPv2 checks the API key in the `x-api-key` header and the token in the `Authorization: Bearer` header, and counts the call against the quota of the project the key belongs to.
{%- elif apiKey and values.enableQuotas %}
2. ESPv2 checks the API key in the `x-api-key` header and counts the call against the quota of the project the key belongs to.
{%- elif apiKey %}
2. ESPv2 checks the API key in the `x-api-key` header.
{%- elif authType != 'none' %}
2. ESPv2 checks the token in the `Authorization: Bearer` header.
{%- else %}
2. ESPv2 accepts the call without credentials.
{%- endif %}
{%- if transcoding %}
3. A JSON request is converted to the gRPC method its path is mapped to; a gRPC request is passed on as it is.
{%- endif %}
{%- if private %}
${{ 4 if transcoding else 3 }}. ESPv2 forwards the request to the backend at `${{ values.backendUrl }}`. No ID token is added; a private address is reached through the Serverless VPC Access connector set in `vpc_connector`.
{%- else %}
${{ 4 if transcoding else 3 }}. ESPv2 forwards the request to the backend at `${{ values.backendUrl }}` with an ID token of the gateway's service account, so the backend can stay closed to anonymous callers.
{%- endif %}
${{ 5 if transcoding else 4 }}. ESPv2 reports the call to Service Control, which writes the request log and the metrics of the API.

{% if grpc %}`${{ package }}.Health.Check`{% else %}`GET /healthz`{% endif %} is exempt from step 2: it needs no credentials{% if values.enableQuotas %} and does not count against the quota{% endif %}.

## The API

The repository starts with a small working definition. Replace it with your own.
{%- if grpc %}

| Method | {% if transcoding %}HTTP mapping | {% endif %}Purpose |
| --- | {% if transcoding %}--- | {% endif %}--- |
| `${{ package }}.Health.Check` | {% if transcoding %}`GET /healthz` | {% endif %}Reports whether the backend answers |
| `${{ package }}.Items.ListItems` | {% if transcoding %}`GET /v1/items` | {% endif %}Returns one page of items |
| `${{ package }}.Items.GetItem` | {% if transcoding %}`GET /v1/items/{item_id}` | {% endif %}Returns one item |

- `${{ values.grpcSpec }}` defines the services and messages. The backend has to implement them.
- `api_config.yaml` is the Endpoints configuration: which services are served, the backend address{% if authType != 'none' %}, the authentication rules{% endif %}{% if values.enableQuotas %}, the quota{% endif %}{% if transcoding %}, and the `http` rules that map HTTP paths to methods{% endif %}. A service or method added to the proto has to be listed under `apis`{% if transcoding %} and, to be reachable over HTTP, under `http`{% endif %}.
- Endpoints needs the proto as a compiled descriptor. The pipelines compile it; see [Deployment](deployment.md#run-terraform-locally) for the command.
{%- else %}

| Operation | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /healthz` | Reports whether the backend answers |
| `GET /v1/items` | Returns one page of items |
| `GET /v1/items/{itemId}` | Returns one item |

Rules for `${{ values.openApiSpec }}`:

- It is an OpenAPI 2.0 (Swagger) document. Cloud Endpoints does not accept OpenAPI 3.
- Every operation needs an `operationId`.
- Values written as `${name}` are filled in by Terraform from `local.api_definition_values` in `infra/main.tf`. Write `$${` for a literal `${`.
{%- if values.enableQuotas %}
- An operation counts against the quota only if it has an `x-google-quota` block.
{%- endif %}
- `python scripts/validate_openapi.py ${{ values.openApiSpec }}` checks the document; the CI workflow runs it on every push.
{%- endif %}

